The legal texts come from ULII, the Uganda Legal Information Institute (Judiciary of Uganda, on the Laws.Africa Peachjam platform):

  ULII, https://ulii.org, point-in-time versions of each act, read from archived copies of its
  pages on the Internet Archive (https://web.archive.org), retrieved 2026-10-28.

Licence and terms, as ULII states them: CC BY-NC 4.0, crediting ULII as the source (https://ulii.org/en/terms-of-use/).
CC BY-NC 4.0: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/legalcode

Attribution:

  Source: ULII, the Uganda Legal Information Institute (https://ulii.org), licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 International licence (CC BY-NC 4.0).

The texts may be used only for non-commercial purposes. This repository is private; whether and how it
may be published is the owner's decision under these terms.

Changes made by Lexifina: each version was converted to one Markdown file per provision, one paragraph per
line; spacing, quotes and dashes were normalised; editorial notes, Part and Chapter headings were left out;
and the versions were arranged as git commits credited to the laws ULII names for each change.
The wording of the law was not changed. Neither ULII nor Laws.Africa endorses this repository.

Since 2026-11-27 the repository also holds Acts of Parliament as enacted and chapters of the Revised Edition of the
Laws of Uganda, 2000, from the PDFs Parliament of Uganda publishes:

  Parliament of Uganda, Acts, https://www.parliament.go.ug/acts (documents served by
  https://cmis.parliament.go.ug), retrieved from 2026-11-27.

These are official texts of the laws of Uganda as published by Parliament; the site states no licence terms for
them. Where a PDF's text layer is the Parliament Library's OCR over page images, it is used as supplied and the
commit says `Text-source: publisher OCR`. Lexifina's changes are as above: one Markdown file per provision, spacing
normalised, marginal notes, Part headings and running headers left out; the wording was not changed.

Some laws not published by Parliament come from FAOLEX, the legislative database of the Food and Agriculture
Organization of the United Nations (https://www.fao.org/faolex/), as a secondary copy; each such commit says
`Source-status: secondary copy (FAOLEX, <record URL>)`. FAO's terms allow non-commercial reuse with credit:

  Source: FAOLEX Database, Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (https://www.fao.org/faolex/).

Lexifina's own contributions (the arrangement of the history, the commit messages, the tables and the
tools) are dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0 Universal:
https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode
